  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Tied into the tenth, Drew Gilbert broke the deadlock with a two-run shot. And that swing matters because it came after the Giants left ten runners on base through nine innings.",
    "what_this_means": "This game was decided by patience, not panic. The lineup couldn't convert early chances. Three runners stranded in the first, two more in the fifth. But they stayed locked in, kept grinding, and found runners on when it mattered. Molina's 5.2 innings of two-hit ball kept them in position. The bullpen held through nine. Then Gilbert made one moment count when another team might have folded. This is what managing a one-run window looks like: you lose the early chances, you survive the middle, and you finish when the next opportunity arrives. They didn't blow the game open. They didn't need to.",
    "what_to_watch": "Tyler Glasnow takes the mound tomorrow at Dodger Stadium with a 3.50 ERA and a lineup that knows how to exploit early mistakes. The kind this team tends to make before the seventh inning.",
    "story_state": {
      "trend": "fragile",
